˜2˜Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial˜Getting Started with a Short Tutorial˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓGetting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, tutorialÏ Ïtutorial, Integrated Development EnvironmentÏ Perhaps the easiest way to understand how the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment manages an entire project's files and tools, is to work through a brief example.  In this walk-through, you create and execute a simple C program using the IDE.  FORTRAN developers can follow along substituting FORTRAN code and file names where appropriate.  The session will help you understand the basic concepts of the IDE and the steps involved in project development.

The illustrations shown in this section are taken from the Windows environment.  The OS/2 environment is very similar with some minor differences.  For example, in the OS/2 environment the IDE uses the OS/2 Editor rather than Open Watcom's Windows Editor.  Although this tutorial builds a Win32 executable, you can choose to build a Win16 or OS/2 PM executable.  The procedures to follow are not that much different.  When the time comes to select the type of target, simply choose an environment that you can run on your machine so that you can run your application to make sure it works.
¥
˜3˜A_Simple_Executable˜A Simple Executable˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓA_Simple_Executable

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, defining a projectÏ

 
Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Double-click on the IDE icon in the Open Watcom Tools window.

The Open Watcom IDE Project window appears.  Ïnew projectÏ

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose New Project from the File menu.

The Enter Project Filename dialog appears.  The default project name isùcourierù12ù noname.wpj.ùhelvù10ù

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Enter the filenameùcourierù12ù \project\firstùhelvù10ù in the File Name field and click on OK.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide4.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 4.ÔibÔÔbÔ Enter the filename in the Enter Project Filename dialog.

This is the name of the project you are creating.  If you enter a directory that doesn't exist (in this case,ùcourierù12ù \projectùhelvù10ù), the IDE reveals a message box asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Click on OK to create the specified directory and project file.

 
ÃNote:
π The IDE looks for existing projects with the new name.  If a project with the specified name already exists, the IDE will prompt you rather than simply overwriting the existing project.
À

The project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Note that the IDE added the extension ".wpj" to the project name.  The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you define a target for the new project.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Enter the desired target name in the Target Name field (we suggestùcourierù12ù target1ùhelvù10ù).  Next, select the appropriate Target Environment (we suggest "Win32") and Image Type (we suggest "Character-mode Executable").  Click on OK when completed.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide5.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 5.ÔibÔÔbÔ Select target name, target environment and image type

The New Target dialog closes and the IDE creates a target window entitledùcourierù12ù target1.exeùhelvù10ù for the new target.  Note that the IDE appends the appropriate extension to the target name for you once you click on OK.  You can now add sources to the target.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Choose New Source from the Sources menu.

The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the sources you want to add to the target.  Source names must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required.

For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename you enter.  You can also use the file browser to add files by clicking on the Browse...  button.

For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field or you can use the file browser to select files.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field or selected a name from the file list, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  All files in the file list can be added by clicking the Add All button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide6.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 6.ÔibÔÔbÔ Select source file names in the Add File(s) dialog

Ã(6)
πÔbÔ Enter the following source file names.

ùcourierù12ù      ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     source1.c   (or source1.for)ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     source2.c   (or source2.for)ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     source3.rcùhelvù10ù

For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space.  Click on OK when done.

For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after each one has been entered.  Click on Close when done.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide7.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 7.ÔibÔÔbÔ Enter one or several source files in the Add File(s) dialog.

The IDE adds the new source files to the target window, followed by the characters [n/a].  This indicates that the source files are not available, meaning that the files do not yet exist.  The indicators are:

 
Ã[n/a]
π This code appears if the file does not exist.

Ã[r/o]
π This code appears if the file is Read Only.

Ã[sw]
π This code appears if the user has set switches for the specified source file.
À

The Integrated Development Environment organizes the source files in the target window under headers according to the filename extension.  You can expand and collapse the file groups by clicking on the folder icon.

For this tutorial, we only need one source file, so now we will delete the extra files from the target.

Ã(7)
πÔbÔ Click on the fileùcourierù12ù source2.cùhelvù10ù (orùcourierù12ù source2.forùhelvù10ù) to select it then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.

A message box appears asking if you want to remove the selected source.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The IDE removes the selected source file from the target window.  Now we can removeùcourierù12ù source3.rc.ùhelvù10ù  To do this we will perform the function of removing all source files with a common extension.

Ã(8)
πÔbÔ Click on the (.rc) header on the target window then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.

A message box appears on the screen asking if you want to remove all of the ".rc" files.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The message box closes and the IDE removes theùcourierù12ù source3.rcùhelvù10ù file from the target window.  Now you can edit the remaining source file.

Ã(9)
πÔbÔ Highlight the lineùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù (orùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù) and choose Edit Text from the Sources menu to edit the selected source file.

The Open Watcom Editor appears.  The name of the selected source file,ùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù (orùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù), appears in the window.  Here you can enter the source code.

ÔibÔHint:  You can also invoke the Open Watcom Editor by double-clicking on the source file that you want to edit.

In this case, the Open Watcom Editor appears because the selected source file has a ".c" or ".for" extension.  Performing an edit can open different editors, depending on the extension of the selected source file.

Ã(10)
πÔbÔ Enter the following lines of source code.

 
ÃImportant:
π For the purpose of this session, we ask you to enterÔbÔ ii instead ofÔbÔ i in the "print" line.  This will allow you to see how the Integrated Development Environment reacts to errors.  You will then be able to correct the error.
À

ùcourierù12ù      ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     #include <stdio.h>ùhelvù10ù

ùcourierù12ù     void main()ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     {ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         int i;ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         for (i=0; i<10; i++)ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         {ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù             printf( "Value is %d\n", ii );ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         }ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         printf("Press Enter...\n");ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù         getchar();ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     }ùhelvù10ù

FORTRAN programmers can enter the following lines of code.

ùcourierù12ù      ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù           program mainùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù           implicit noneùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù           integer iùhelvù10ù

ùcourierù12ù           do i = 0, 9ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù             print *, 'Value is ', iiùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù           enddoùhelvù10ù

ùcourierù12ù           print *, 'Press Enter...'ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù           read *ùhelvù10ù

ùcourierù12ù           endùhelvù10ù

You can now save your work and close the Editor in one of the following two ways:  close down the entire editor or close down only the source file window.  For this exercise, close only the source file window as follows.

Ã(11)
πÔbÔ Choose Close from the File menu to close down the current source file window.

In both cases, if changes have been made to the file and not saved, a message prompts you to save the changes before exiting.  The advantage of closing only the Editor window is that the Editor continues to run.  If you need to access it again, you will not need to wait for it to go through the start-up procedures again.

 
ÃImportant:
π You must save the file.  The Open Watcom compilers do not access the source lines in the Editor's buffers.
À

Ã(12)
πÔbÔ Minimize the Editor window.

or

Ã 
πÔbÔ Click on the Open Watcom IDE Project window to bring it to the foreground.

Ã(13)
πÔbÔ Choose Refresh from the Window menu.

This action forces the IDE to update the display with the state of all files.  Ïauto refreshÏ It is not necessary to use this function if you have selected Auto Refresh in the File menu.  If Auto Refresh is disabled then it may be necessary to alert the IDE of the presence of the file on disk.  Ïsave projectÏ

Ã(14)
πÔbÔ Choose Save Project from the File menu to save your project up to this point.

You are now ready to make and run the target.  Making a target is the act of constructing a target file.  In this case, it involves compiling the source fileùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù (orùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù) using the appropriate Open Watcom compiler and linking the target executable using the Open Watcom Linker.

Ã(15)
πÔbÔ Highlight the lineùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù and choose C Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.

ÔbÔFORTRAN programmers:  Highlight the lineùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù and choose Fortran Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.

The C Compiler Switches dialog appears.  If you are using FORTRAN, the Fortran Compiler Switches dialog appears.

Ã(16)
πÔbÔ Click on the ">>" button until theùcourierù12ù 7.  CodeGen Strategy Switchesùhelvù10ù dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox button to select this option.

ÔbÔFORTRAN programmers:  Click on the ">>" button until theùcourierù12ù 9.  Application Type Switchesùhelvù10ù dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox to select this option.

A check-mark appears in the checkbox.

Note:  Selecting this option is for demonstration purposes only, it is not required for this simple application.

Click OK to close the dialog.

Ã(17)
πÔbÔ Choose Make from the Targets menu.

The IDE constructs a makefile based on its knowledge of the target and construction rules and then invokes the Open Watcom Make utility to createùcourierù12ù target1.exe.ùhelvù10ù  The IDE Log window appears at the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Use this window to monitor the procedure.  Since you enteredÔbÔ ii instead ofÔbÔ i, the MAKE fails.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide8.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 8.ÔibÔÔbÔ Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.

Ã(18)
πÔbÔ Highlight the error message in the IDE Log window and choose Help on Message from the Log menu.
Help information regarding the error message appears on the screen.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide9.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 9.ÔibÔÔbÔ Help for error messages is available

Close the Help window and edit the source file as follows.

Ã(19)
πÔbÔ Highlight the error message in the IDE Log window and choose Edit File from the Log menu.

or

Ã 
πÔbÔ Double-click on the error message in the IDE Log window.

The Open Watcom Editor opens and the cursor appears at the position of the error.  Edit and correct the error and then save the file again.  Minimize the Editor window and bring the IDE window to the foreground.

Ã(20)
πÔbÔ Choose Make from the Targets menu again.

This time, the procedure creates the executable fileùcourierù12ù target1.exe.ùhelvù10ù

Ã(21)
πÔbÔ Choose Run from the Targets menu.

The IDE executes the programùcourierù12ù target1.exe.ùhelvù10ù

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide10.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 10.ÔibÔÔbÔ Run from the Targets menu runs the target.

The program you wrote is a character mode application and its output appears in a separate console window.  You are now ready to debug your target program.  You probably do not need to debug this simple program with the Open Watcom Debugger but go through the steps so that you know what to do for more complex programs.

The IDE has several debugging information options which it uses as defaults when it is started.  The first thing to do is check to make sure that your application compiled with the default debugging information activated.

Ã(22)
πÔbÔ Choose Target Options from the Targets menu to activate the Target Options menu.

The option Use Development Switches should appear in grey text with a check mark next to it.  Development Switches automatically include debugging information in the target.  If you were to select Use Release Switches, the IDE would select options useful for a production version of your program.  Selecting this option turns the text grey and places a check mark beside it.  The IDE uses the development switches by default.

 
ÃNote:
π For the purpose of this session, you should make the target with the development switches on.  If this is not the option selected, select it and remake the target before debugging.
À

Ã(23)
πÔbÔ Choose Debug from the Targets menu.

The IDE starts the Open Watcom Debugger.

Ã(24)
πÔbÔ Choose Step Over from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to step through the code.  ChooseÔbÔ Go from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to continue execution to the end of the code.  For more information on using the Open Watcom Debugger, refer to theÔibÔ Open Watcom Debugger User's Guide.ÔbÔ  The task has completed running appears when the program has completed execution.

Ã(25)
πÔbÔ Choose Exit from the File menu to terminate the debugging session.

The Open Watcom Debugger closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now exit the IDE.

Ã(26)
πÔbÔ Choose Exit from the File menu to exit the IDE.

The IDE prompts you to save the current project if you have not saved it since the last change.
À
