˜3˜Review_of_What_Happened˜Review of What Happened˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓReview_of_What_Happened

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, tutorial reviewÏ In this session, you created a project calledùcourierù12ù first.wpj.ùhelvù10ù  This project had one target calledùcourierù12ù target1.exe,ùhelvù10ù a 32-bit Windows executable (unless you specified another target type).  Using the Open Watcom Editor, you entered and edited the source text in a file calledùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù orùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù (if you tried the FORTRAN example).  You compiled and linked it into an executable program using the Open Watcom Make utility, a Open Watcom compiler, and the Open Watcom Linker.  You ran it directly and under the control of the Open Watcom Debugger.

When you saved the project, the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment created the following permanent files:

 
Ãfirst.wpj
π Describes the screen layout and refers to a file calledùcourierù12ù target1.tgtùhelvù10ù

Ãtarget1.tgt
π Describes the target executableùcourierù12ù target1.exeùhelvù10ù and all switches required to link it.  It also describesùcourierù12ù source1.cùhelvù10ù (orùcourierù12ù source1.forùhelvù10ù) and the switches required to compile it.
À
¥
˜2˜The_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window˜The Open Watcom IDE Project Window˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓThe_Open_Watcom_IDE_Project_Window

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, project windowÏ Ïproject window, Integrated Development EnvironmentÏ When you first start the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, an empty Open Watcom IDE Project window opens.  This window represents the project that is currently open in the IDE.  The name of the current project appears in the title bar of this window.

The first thing you need to do is open a project for the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  This can be a new project you create or an existing project you open.

 
ÃImportant:
π You can have only one project open at a time in the IDE.  If you attempt to open or create a project while one already exists in the IDE window, a message box appears informing you that the IDE is closing the current project and asks if you want to save any changes made to that project.  Choose Yes, No, or Cancel in this box to continue.
À
¥
˜3˜Opening_a_Project˜Opening a Project˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓOpening_a_Project

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, opening a projectÏ Ïopening a projectÏ

 
ÃTo create a new project:
π  Ïnew projectÏ

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose New Project from the File menu.

The Enter Project Filename dialog appears on the screen where you enter the name of the project you are creating.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Enter the desired name in the File Name field and choose the desired directory.

All projects must have the extension ".wpj".

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide11.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 11.ÔibÔÔbÔ Use the Enter Project Filename dialog to create a new project.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Click on OK when completed.

The Enter Project Filename dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  A New Target dialog opens on the screen where you add a target to the new project.  Refer to the section Adding a Target for more information.
À

 
ÃTo open an existing project:
π  Ïopen projectÏ

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Open Project from the File menu.

The Open dialog appears on the screen where you select the project you want to open.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide12.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 12.ÔibÔÔbÔ Use the Open dialog to open an existing project.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose the desired project.

All projects have the extension ".wpj".

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Click on OK when the desired project appears in the File Name field.

The Open dialog closes and the name of the selected project appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  If targets exist for the selected project, windows representing the targets appear in the main window.
À
¥
˜3˜Closing_a_Project˜Closing a Project˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓClosing_a_Project

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, closing a projectÏ Ïclosing a projectÏ Ïclose projectÏ

 
ÃTo close the current project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Close Project from the File menu.

The IDE removes all target windows for the current project from the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The project name disappears from the title bar and the window is empty.
À
¥
˜3˜Saving_a_Project˜Saving a Project˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓSaving_a_Project

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, saving a projectÏ Ïsaving a projectÏ There are two ways of saving your current project.  You can perform a simple save where the project is updated with any changes made since the last save or you can save the project under a different project name using the Save Project As function.
¥
˜4˜Updating_the_Project˜Updating the Project˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓUpdating_the_Project

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, updating a projectÏ Ïupdating a projectÏ Ïsave projectÏ

 
ÃTo perform a simple save on your project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Save Project from the File menu.

The IDE updates the project specified in the title bar.  The status bar along the bottom of the window indicates that the save function is being executed.
À
¥
˜4˜Saving_the_Project_Under_a_New_Name˜Saving the Project Under a New Name˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓSaving_the_Project_Under_a_New_Name

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, saving a project under a new nameÏ Ïsaving a project under a new nameÏ Ïsave project asÏ

 
ÃTo save the current project using a different name:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Save Project As from the File menu.

The Save As dialog appears on the screen where you specify the file name by which you want to save the current project.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Enter the desired project name in the File Name field.

This is the new name by which you want to save the current project.  This can be the name of a new project or an existing project.

 
ÃImportant:
π Entering the name of an existing project overwrites the contents of that project with the contents of the current project.
À

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose the desired directory for the project using the Directories list on the Save As dialog.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Click on OK when completed.

The Save As dialog closes and the new project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
À
¥
˜2˜Working_with_Targets˜Working with Targets˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓWorking_with_Targets

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, targetsÏ ÏtargetsÏ Once you have created a project, it is a simple matter to add additional targets.  You can also change the name of a target, remove targets, change the target environment, and change the image type.  This section discusses the following topics:

“c
” Adding a Target

” Minimizing a Target

” Removing a Target

” Renaming a Target

” Marking a Target for Remake

” Making Targets

” Running a Target
‘
¥
˜3˜Adding_a_Target˜Adding a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓAdding_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, adding a targetÏ Ïtargets, addingÏ You add targets to the current project using the New Target dialog.  When you create a new project, this dialog appears automatically.  You have two options when adding targets to a project:  you can either enter the name of a new target or you can choose an existing target, previously created in another project.  This section describes both methods for adding a target.

 
ÃTo add a new target to the current project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose New Target from the Targets menu.

The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you enter the details of the target you are adding.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide13.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 13.ÔibÔÔbÔ Enter the new target details in the New Target dialog.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Enter the target name in the Target Name field.

Enter the entire pathname to ensure the directory is created where you want.  Depending on the nature of the target, it may or may not be able to share the same directory as other pre-existing targets.  Generally, if the target shares one or more of the same source files as other targets in the project, then it must be placed in its own subdirectory.  The object files that are produced for the target may have different attributes than a pre-existing target's object files (e.g., 16-bit vs 32-bit).  This is why the target should be placed in a separate subdirectory.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Select the desired Target Environment and Image Type as desired.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Click on OK when the Target Name, Target Environment and Image Type fields are correct.

If you specify a new directory in the target pathname, a message box appears asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Choose Yes or No to continue.  A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.

The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode, the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in Development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
À

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide14.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 14.ÔibÔÔbÔ A project may have multiple targets.

 
ÃTo add an existing target to a current project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose New Target from the Targets menu.

The New Target dialog appears on the screen.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Click on the Browse button.

The Enter Target Filename dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and files and select the ".tgt" file for the target you want to add.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide15.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 15.ÔibÔÔbÔ Choose an existing target from the Pick Target Filename dialog for the current project.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Click on the desired target to select it.

The selected target appears in the File Name field.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Click on OK to process the target selection.

The Enter Target Filename dialog closes.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Click on OK to close the New Target dialog.

A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.

The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode and the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
À
¥
˜3˜Minimizing_a_Target˜Minimizing a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓMinimizing_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, minimizing a targetÏ Ïminimizing a targetÏ If you are working with a project that has multiple targets, the Open Watcom IDE Project window can become cluttered.  You can tidy your window by minimizing the target windows that you are not currently using.

 
ÃTo minimize a target window:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click on the system box in the upper left-hand corner of the target window you want to close.

A menu appears listing the functions you can perform on the selected window.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Minimize from the menu.

The selected target window closes and an icon appears along the bottom of the screen representing that window.  The name of the target appears on or under the icon.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Double-click on the icon to open the target window again.
À

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide16.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 16.ÔibÔÔbÔ Minimize targets to reduce clutter in the project window.
¥
˜3˜Removing_a_Target˜Removing a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓRemoving_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, removing a targetÏ Ïremoving a targetÏ The procedure for removing a target from a project is simple.  If your project has multiple targets, it is important to ensure that the target you want to remove is the one selected.  Removing a target does not permanently delete the target, it simply removes it from the current project.  If the target is attached to multiple projects, those projects will not be affected by the removal of the selected target from the current project.

 
ÃTo remove a target from the current project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window you want to remove from the project.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Remove Target from the Targets menu.

A message box appears on the screen asking if you really want to remove the selected target.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose OK to continue.

The message box closes and the selected target window disappears from the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
À
¥
˜3˜Renaming_a_Target˜Renaming a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓRenaming_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, renaming a targetÏ Ïrenaming a targetÏ When renaming a target, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing target.  Choosing an existing target name overwrites the contents of the existing target with the contents of the current target.

 
ÃTo rename a target:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window you want to rename.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Rename Target from the Targets menu.

The Rename Target dialog appears.  The Target Name, Target Environment, and Image Type of the selected target appear in the appropriate fields.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide17.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 17.ÔibÔÔbÔ Change a target's name using the Rename Target dialog.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Enter the new target name in the Target Name field.

or

Ã 
πÔbÔ Click on the Browse button and select an existing target name; click on OK when completed.

The selected or entered target name appears in the Target Name field.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Verify that the Target Environment and Image Type are correct.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Click on OK on the Rename Target window.

The Rename Target dialog closes.  The title bar of the selected target window now displays the new target name.
À
¥
˜3˜Marking_a_Target_for_Remake˜Marking a Target for Remake˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓMarking_a_Target_for_Remake

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, marking a targetÏ Ïmarking a targetÏ Marking a target for remake ensures that the IDE will perform the link the next time you make that target or make all targets.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Setup Target submenu under the Targets menu.  By marking a target for remake, you control when and which targets need to be remade.

 
ÃTo mark a target for remake:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window you want to mark.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Mark Target for Remake from the Targets menu.

A message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the selected target.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose Yes or No to continue.

Choosing Yes marks all of the source files for the selected target as well as the target itself.  This means that the next time you do a make, the source files for the selected target will be compiled.  Choosing No marks only the selected target for remake.  In both cases, the message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
À
¥
˜3˜Making_Targets˜Making Targets˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓMaking_Targets

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, making a targetÏ Ïmaking a targetÏ The Make function in the Targets menu creates an executable for the selected target.  When you perform this function, the IDE compiles the source files for the selected target into object files.  The object files are then linked together to create the target file.

There are two make functions available to you for targets:  Make and Make All.  The Make All function in the Actions menu tells the IDE to compile and link all of the targets for the current project.  This can be a lengthy process, depending upon the number of targets in your project, so you may choose to only make targets to which changes have been made.  This enables you to see if they can be made successfully.

Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make.

 
ÃTo make a single target:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window you want to make to select it.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Make from the Targets menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ÔbÔ  Execution Complete appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected target.  If the make is successful an executable file for the selected target can be found in the directory associated with the target window.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide18.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 18.ÔibÔÔbÔ Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.

À

 
ÃTo make all targets for the current project:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Make All from the Actions Menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ÔbÔ  Execution Complete appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets.  If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
À
¥
˜4˜Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target˜Viewing the Make Command for a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓViewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, viewing make commandsÏ Ïviewing make commandsÏ With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected target.  These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected target when you choose the function Make from the Targets menu or Make All from the Actions menu.

 
ÃTo view the make commands for targets:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window whose makefile commands you want to view.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Target Options from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears on the screen from the Target Options menu.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose Show Make Command from the Target Options submenu.

The Target Command dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide19.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 19.ÔibÔÔbÔ The Target Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Click on OK to close the dialog.
À
¥
˜3˜Running_a_Target˜Running a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓRunning_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, running a targetÏ Ïrunning a targetÏ Once you have compiled and linked your target using the Make function, you can run the target to execute the program.

 
ÃTo run a target:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target window you want to run.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Run from the Targets menu.

The IDE executes the target program.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide20.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 20.ÔibÔÔbÔ Run from the Targets menu runs the target.

