˜3˜Using_the_Autodepend_Switch˜Using the Autodepend Switch˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓUsing_the_Autodepend_Switch

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, autodepend switchÏ Ïswitches, autodependÏ The Use Autodepend switch in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu keeps track of updated source files.  Once a source is modified, the IDE remembers that it needs to be recompiled the next time you do a make.  By using the Use Autodepend switch, the IDE also keeps track of changes made to source files on which other source files depend.

For example, let's say that your current project has a source file,ùcourierù12ù source.cpp,ùhelvù10ù that depends on a header file,ùcourierù12ù source.hpp.ùhelvù10ù  If the Use Autodepend feature is not enabled and changes are made toùcourierù12ù source.hppùhelvù10ù afterùcourierù12ù source.cppùhelvù10ù in your current project has been compiled, the IDE does not recognize that your current source file is now out of date.  This means that the next time you do a make, the IDE will not compileùcourierù12ù source.cpp.ùhelvù10ù  The Use Autodepend feature tells the IDE to compare the date and time stamps on all of the dependent source files in addition to the source files in the current project and their corresponding object files, so the IDE recognizes when your current source file needs to be recompiled.

 
ÃTo set the Use Autodepend switch:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Target Options from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Select the Use Autodepend option from the Target Options submenu.

If the option was enabled when you selected it, you have disabled the Use Autodepend feature.  If the option had no check mark when you selected it, you have enabled the Use Autodepend feature.
À
¥
˜2˜Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment˜Configuring the Integrated Development Environment˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓConfiguring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, configurationÏ Ïconfiguring the IDEÏ ÏIntegrated Development Environment, customizationÏ Ïcustomizing the IDEÏ The Integrated Development Environment can be reconfigured to some extent.  You can customize the following features.

“c
” Show or hide the Toolbar

” Show or hide the Status Bar

” Enable or disable automatic updates of file status in the target windows

” Select your own text editor

” Select a source revision control system
‘

The next few sections describe these features.
¥
˜3˜Show_Toolbar˜Show Toolbar˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓShow_Toolbar

Ïshow tool barÏ The Integrated Development Environment Toolbar that appears under the Menu Bar can be removed from or added to the IDE window.

 
ÃTo add or remove the toolbar:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose File from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Show Tool Bar indicates that the toolbar is present on the screen.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Select Show Tool Bar.

The Show Tool Bar state will be toggled.  If the toolbar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the toolbar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
À
¥
˜3˜Show_Status_Bar˜Show Status Bar˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓShow_Status_Bar

Ïshow status barÏ The Integrated Development Environment Status Bar that appears at the bottom of the IDE window can be removed from or added to the screen.

 
ÃTo add or remove the status bar:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose File from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Show Status Bar indicates that the status bar is present on the screen.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Select Show Status Bar.

The Show Status Bar state will be toggled.  If the status bar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the status bar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
À
¥
˜3˜Auto_Refresh˜Auto Refresh˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓAuto_Refresh

Ïauto refreshÏ The IDE will automatically update the target windows with the state of all files when this option is enabled.

 
ÃTo enable or disable auto refresh:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose File from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Auto Refresh indicates that auto refresh is enabled.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Select Auto Refresh.

The Auto Refresh state will be toggled.  When Auto Refresh is disabled, it may be necessary to select Refresh from the Window menu whenever the target window status becomes out of date.
À
¥
˜3˜Selecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor˜Selecting Your Own Text Editor˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓSelecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor

Ïset text editorÏ You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Integrated Development Environment.

 
ÃTo select your own text editor:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Set Text Editor from the File menu.

The Set Text Editor dialog appears.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field.  You must also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL).  If the editor is an executable file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field.  The argument line will be supplied to the editor whenever it is started by the IDE.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will be filled in by the IDE.  The macros are:

 
Ã%f
π The name of the file to be edited.

Ã%r
π The row in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1.

Ã%c
π The column in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1.
À

For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was:

ùcourierù12ù      ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     file='%f' row='%r' col='%c'ùhelvù10ù

and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the fileùcourierù12ù foobar.cùhelvù10ù with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed to your editor is:

ùcourierù12ù      ùhelvù10ù
ùcourierù12ù     file='foobar.c' row='215' col='31'ùhelvù10ù

This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position the text cursor.  If no row or column is available, then the IDE will supply the value of 1 as a default.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Select OK when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor.

or

Ã 
πÔbÔ Select Cancel when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor.

or

Ã 
πÔbÔ Select Default when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel.
À
¥
˜3˜Selecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System˜Selecting a Source Revision Control System˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓSelecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System

Ïset source controlÏ ÏRCSÏ You can use a source code Revision Control System (RCS) in conjunction with the Integrated Development Environment.

 
ÃTo select a revision control system
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Choose Set Source Control from the File menu.

A number of options are presented:

 
ÃMKS RCS
π ÏMKS RCSÏ Mortice Kern Systems Revision Control System

ÃMKS SI
π ÏMKS SIÏ Mortice Kern Systems Source Integrity

ÃPVCS
π ÏPVCSÏ Intersolv Project Version Control System

ÃOther
π Ïgeneric RCSÏ Any other flavour of revision control system (default)

ÃNone
π No revision control system

ÃObject Cycle
π ÏObject CycleÏ Powersoft's Object Cycle

ÃPerforce
π ÏPerforceÏ Perforce
À

À

Ïcheckout sourceÏ Ïcheckin sourceÏ When you select a source revision control system, the Checkout Source and Checkin Source items in the Sources menu are enabled.  The Checkout Source and Checkin Source items spawn batch/command files or dialogs corresponding to your selection of revision control system.  The batch/command files are located in the \WATCOM\BINW and \WATCOM\BINP directories.  These files can be modified to suit your environment.

 
ÃMKS RCS
π

 
ÃMKS_CO
π for checking out/locking files with MKS RCS

ÃMKS_CI
π for checking in/unlocking files with MKS RCS
À

ÃMKS SI
π

 
ÃCheckout Dialog
π a check out dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI

ÃCheckin Dialog
π a check in dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI
À

ÃPVCS
π

 
ÃPVCS_CO
π for checking out/locking files with Intersolv PVCS

ÃPVCS_CI
π for checking in/unlocking files with Intersolv PVCS
À

ÃOther
π

 
ÃGEN_CO
π a file which you can edit to provide check out (and lock) functionality to any generic revision control system

ÃGEN_CI
π a file which you can edit to provide check in (and unlock) functionality to any generic revision control system
À

ÃNone
π When selected, Checkout Source and Checkin Source are disabled.
À

When one of the batch files is spawned, a number of arguments are passed to the batch file.

For checking out files, the following arguments are passed:

»
” "pause" or "nopause" to enable optional screen pause after completion of action

” full source file path specification

” full project file path specification (.wpj file)

” full target file path specification (.tgt file)
…

For checking in files, the following arguments are passed:

»
” full source file path specification

” full message file path specification (this file contains the textual message that you enter when checking in a file)

” full project file path specification (.wpj file)

” full target file path specification (.tgt file)
…
¥
˜2˜Executing_Special_Commands˜Executing Special Commands˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓExecuting_Special_Commands

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, before makeÏ ÏbeforeÏ Ïbefore makeÏ ÏIntegrated Development Environment, after makeÏ ÏafterÏ Ïafter makeÏ Sometimes you may find it necessary to execute commands before and/or after performing a make.  The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment provides Before and After commands in the File and Targets menus.  These commands enable you to perform actions immediately before or after making either the selected target or all targets for the current project.

 
ÃTo set Before or After directives:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Click once on the target for which you want to set a before or after directive.

The title bar of the selected target is highlighted.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Choose Target Options from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose Execute Before or Execute After from the Target Options submenu.

A Target Before or Target After dialog appears on your screen.

 
ÃNote:
π If you want to set before or after directives to be executed when you make all targets, select the before and after commands from the File menu.
À

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Enter the command you want to execute before or after the selected target is made.

ùcourierù12ù∞c∞ide29.bmp∞ùhelvù10ù
ÔibÔFigure 29.ÔibÔÔbÔ Enter execute commands in the Target Before or Target After dialogs.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Click on OK to continue.

The IDE stores the information you entered and closes the dialog.  When you next perform a make on the selected target, the IDE will execute the before or after directive you entered.
À
¥
˜2˜Adding_Resources_to_a_Target˜Adding Resources to a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓAdding_Resources_to_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, adding resourcesÏ Ïresources, addingÏ You may want to include resources in your target executable.  There are two ways of doing this in the IDE:  indirectly and directly.  The act of adding resources to a target can only be done for targets that support resources, such as Windows or OS/2 PM.

 
ÃTo add a resource to a target using the indirect method:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Create a new source file with the extension ".rc".

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Click once on the new source file to select it.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose Edit Text from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Editor.

The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now add the resource file text.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Enter the resource statements in the editor.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Save your work and close the editor when you are finished.

You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.

Ã(6)
πÔbÔ Click once on the new source file to select it.

The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.

Ã(7)
πÔbÔ Choose Make from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.

The IDE compiles the selected source file.  To do this, the IDE invokes the Resource Compiler which creates a ".res" file.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
À

When you create the resource file directly, the source file contains the same information but it is stored in a different format.

 
ÃTo add a resource to a target using the direct method:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Create a new source file with the extension ".res".

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Click once on the new source file to select it.

Ã(3)
πÔbÔ Choose Edit Resources from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Resource Editor.

The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now create the resources.

Ã(4)
πÔbÔ Create the resources.

Ã(5)
πÔbÔ Save your work and close the editor when you are finished.

You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.

Ã(6)
πÔbÔ Click once on the new source file to select it.

The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.

Ã(7)
πÔbÔ Choose Make from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.

The IDE compiles the selected source file.  The IDE does not need to invoke the Resource Compiler to create a ".res" file because you created the ".res" file directly using the Resource Editor.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
À
¥
˜2˜Including_Libraries_in_a_Target˜Including Libraries in a Target˜Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
ÓIncluding_Libraries_in_a_Target

ÏIntegrated Development Environment, adding librariesÏ Ïlibraries, addingÏ You use the same procedure to include static and DLL libraries in your target as you use to add any source file to a target.  The IDE includes the libraries when the target is linked.

 
ÃTo create static and DLL libraries:
π 

Ã(1)
πÔbÔ Create a new target entering the library name in the Target Name field and choosing the appropriate image type.

Clicking on OK on the New Target dialog closes the dialog and creates a new target with the specified name in the title bar.

Ã(2)
πÔbÔ Add the desired source files, new or existing, to the library you have created.

 
ÃNote:
π If you want one of your other targets to use that library, you must enter the library as a source file for that target.  When you enter the library as a source file you must give it the extension ".lib", regardless of whether the library is a static or DLL library.  For example, if your library is entitled Target A and you want your existing Target B to use that library, add Target A as a source file on Target B with the extension ".lib".
À

À
¥
